Q: How can I see the command history across all PowerShell sessions in Windows Server 2016? A: <p>I appear to be very late to this party, but thought I'd throw this into the mix and the solution that got me as close as I can to persistent history list with ! type functionality.</p>
<p>Test if you have a $PROFILE: Test-Path $PROFILE</p>
<p>If False create $PROFILE: New-Item -ItemType File -Path $PROFILE -Force</p>
<p>Edit profile: notepad $PROFILE</p>
<p>Add the following to your $PROFILE in notepad:</p>
<pre><code>Set-PSReadLineOption -HistorySaveStyle SaveIncrementally
Set-PSReadLineOption -MaximumHistoryCount 10000
Set-PSReadLineOption -HistoryNoDuplicates
Set-PSReadLineOption -HistorySearchCursorMovesToEnd

function Get-CleanHistory {
    $raw = Get-Content (Get-PSReadLineOption).HistorySavePath
    $merged = @()
    $buffer = $null
    foreach ($line in $raw) {
        $buffer = if ($null -ne $buffer) { &quot;$buffer`n$line&quot; } else { $line }
        if ($buffer.TrimEnd().EndsWith('`')) {
            $buffer = $buffer.TrimEnd().TrimEnd('`')   # strip continuation marker, keep buffering
        } else {
            $merged += $buffer
            $buffer = $null
        }
    }
    if ($buffer) { $merged += $buffer }
    $merged | Get-Unique
}

function rh {
    param([int]$Id)
    $lines = Get-CleanHistory
    if ($Id -lt 1 -or $Id -gt $lines.Count) {
        Write-Warning &quot;No such history entry: $Id&quot;
        return
    }
    $cmd = $lines[$Id - 1]
    Write-Host &quot;&gt; $cmd&quot; -ForegroundColor Cyan
    Invoke-Expression $cmd
}

function hist {
    Get-CleanHistory | ForEach-Object -Begin {$i=1} -Process { &quot;{0,4}  {1}&quot; -f $i,$_; $i++ }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Save and exit notepad.</p>
<p>Load edited $PROFILE: . $PROFILE</p>
<p>If that fails, update execution policy: Set-ExecutionPolicy RemoteSigned -Scope CurrentUser</p>
<p>Load edited $PROFILE: . $PROFILE</p>
<p>You now have hist (instead of history)<br />
You can run previous commands using: rh {number}</p>
